            <div xml:id="d356">

                <pb facs="0373" n="257" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg257"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 839.00/1628a.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Chargé White.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-25T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 25, 1914, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Please advise President Báez to proceed on the theory that Congress will assemble
                    and permit the election of President Jiménez to be proclaimed and let him accept
                    the oath of office in the presence of the National Assembly. It would be well
                    for him in conversation to avoid expressing any doubt as to the willingness of
                    the opposition to join in putting the election into effect. If when the time
                    comes the opposition refuses to attend and this refusal continues for several
                    days, that is, long enough to make it certain that the opposition is trying to
                    obstruct the will of the people as expressed by the popular election in the
                    electoral college and in the Assembly, this Government will advise further in
                    regard to the steps necessary to be taken. You may assure President Báez of this
                    Government’s intention of keeping its promise to furnish such assistance as may
                    be necessary to maintain an orderly Government now that the will of the people
                    has been freely and clearly expressed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
